      "role": "user",
      "content": "What are the most common mistakes or pitfalls here?",
      "ts": "2026-05-19T12:49:59.942568+00:00"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the study of probability and distributions\u2014especially when applied to quantitative finance\u2014there are a few classic pitfalls that frequently trip up students. Here are the most common ones:\n\n**1. Assuming \\(\\mathbb{E}[g(X)] = g(\\mathbb{E}[X])\\)**\nStudents often incorrectly assume they can easily swap functions and expectations. In general, the expected value of a function of a random variable is *not* equal to the function of the expected value [1, 2]. This is only consistently true if \\(g\\) is a linear function [3, 4]. \n\n**2. Believing Zero Covariance Implies Independence**\nWhile it is true that independent random variables always have a covariance of zero, the reverse is false [5, 6]. Variables can have zero covariance but still be entirely dependent on one another (for example, if \\(Y = X^2\\) and \\(X\\) is symmetrically distributed around zero) [7, 8]. Covariance only measures *linear* dependence.\n\n**3. Blindly Applying the Central Limit Theorem (CLT)**\nThe CLT is universally powerful, but it strictly requires the underlying random variables to have a finite mean and a finite variance [9, 10]. In quantitative finance, we often encounter distributions with \"fat tails\" (power-law distributions) where higher moments, or even the variance itself, diverge [11, 12]. When variance does not exist, the CLT scaling argument fails and distributions do not converge to a Gaussian [10, 13].\n\n**4. The Inspection Paradox (Random Incidence)**\nWhen studying Poisson processes, a common mistake is neglecting sample bias. For instance, if buses arrive as a Poisson process every 15 minutes on average, you might expect the time between buses to still average 15 minutes if you arrive at the stop at a random time [14, 15]. In reality, arriving at a random time makes you disproportionately more likely to fall into a larger inter-arrival interval, pushing the expected interval you observe to 30 minutes [16, 17]. \n\nWould you like to look at a specific mathematical example of one of these pitfalls, or should we move on to testing some of these concepts with a practice problem?",
